.TH ADSSH 1 "2026" "adssh" "User Commands"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH NAME
adssh \- programmable DevOps shell (Starlark + POSIX shell hybrid)
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adssh
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fIscript.star|script.adssh\fR]
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B adssh
is a security-first programmable shell that blends a Starlark (safe Python
dialect) REPL with a full POSIX shell interpreter.
It provides RBAC entitlements, OPA/Rego policy enforcement, structured
audit logging, a built-in SSH server, and a suite of virtual binaries
(\fBjq\fR, \fByq\fR, \fBhttp\fR, \fBcmdgen\fR, and more) — all without
installing external tools.
.PP
When invoked interactively,
.B adssh
presents a dual-mode REPL.
Lines that look like Starlark are evaluated as Starlark; lines that look
like shell commands are routed to the embedded POSIX shell interpreter.
Use
.B !
or
.B $
as a prefix to force shell mode.
.PP
When a
.I script.star
argument is supplied, the file is evaluated as Starlark non-interactively and
adssh exits.
When a
.I script.adssh
argument is supplied, the file is evaluated line-by-line through the same
shell, policy, audit, elevation, lease, gateway, and recording path used by
interactive commands.
Prefix a
.I script.adssh
line with
.B -
to mark an expected failure and continue to the next line.
Shebang lines (\fB#!\fR) are stripped automatically.
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Treat this session as a login shell.
Causes
.B ~/.adsshprofile
(or the path in \fBADSSH_PROFILE\fR) to be sourced before the RC file.
This flag is also set automatically when
.B argv[0]
starts with
.BR \- .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
Enable restricted (sandboxed) mode.
In restricted mode path traversal, \fBcd\fR, \fBexport\fR, and other
privileged shell operations are blocked by the security layer.
.TP
.BI \-c " cmd"
Evaluate
.I cmd
as a single expression or command and exit.
The string is parsed with the same mode-detection heuristic used in the
REPL (Starlark first, then POSIX shell).
.BI \-\-serve " :PORT"
Start the built-in SSH server listening on
.I :PORT
(e.g.
.BR \-\-serve\ :2222 ).
The server reads host keys from
.B ~/.adssh/host_key
and authorised keys from
.B ~/.adssh/authorized_keys
(overridable via environment variables).
The process remains in the foreground; combine with
.BR \-\-login / \-\-restricted
to control the session policy for remote users.
The server also authorizes SSH
.B direct-tcpip
channels, so it can act as a policy-gated jump host.
.TP
.BI \-\-entitlements " PATH"
Load RBAC entitlements from the YAML file at
.IR PATH .
Entitlements control which roles and capabilities are available to
authenticated users.
.TP
.BI \-\-policy " PATH"
Load an OPA/Rego policy file from
.IR PATH .
The policy is evaluated for every command before execution; a deny decision
terminates the command with an error.
.TP
.B \-\-init
Create
.B ~/.adssh/
with a starter
.BR authorized_keys ,
.BR default.rego ,
and
.B .adsshrc
file, then exit.
Existing files are not overwritten.
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH IDENTITY AND ADMIN COMMANDS
.B identity
imports externally verified OIDC claims into the current session, maps group
claims into policy principals, and can create a local SSH CA or issue
short-lived SSH user certificates.
.PP
.B admin
exposes a governed local API for sessions, gateways, pending approvals,
explanations, and evidence export.
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_LOG
Path to the append-only audit log file.
Default:
.BR ~/.adssh/audit.log .
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_URL
Webhook URL for streaming audit events to a remote SIEM.
When set, every audit event is also POSTed to this endpoint.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_TOKEN
Bearer token sent in the
.B Authorization
header when
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_URL
is configured.
.TP
.B ADSSH_POLICY
Path to the OPA/Rego policy file.
Default:
.BR ~/.adssh/policy.rego .
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-policy .
.TP
.B ADSSH_ENTITLEMENTS
Path to the RBAC entitlements YAML file.
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-entitlements .
.TP
.B ADSSH_RESTRICTED
Set to
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
or
.B yes
to enable restricted mode at startup.
Equivalent to
.BR \-r .
.TP
.B ADSSH_SERVE
SSH server listen address (e.g.
.BR :2222 ).
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-serve .
.TP
.B ADSSH_MODEL
Name of the AI model used by Starlark AI builtins (e.g.
.BR claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022 ).
.TP
.B ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
API key for the Anthropic Claude API, used by AI-powered virtual binaries
and Starlark extensions.
.TP
.B ADSSH_PROFILE
Path to the login profile script (Starlark).
Default:
.BR ~/.adsshprofile .
Loaded only in login shells.
.TP
.B ADSSH_RC
Path to the interactive RC script (Starlark).
Default:
.BR ~/.adsshrc .
Loaded for every interactive session.
.TP
.B ADSSH_HISTORY
Path to the readline history file.
Default:
.BR ~/.adssh/history .
.TP
.B ADSSH_RECORD_DIR
Directory for JSONL session recordings.
Default:
.BR ~/.adssh/recordings .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for user configuration files.
.B adssh
respects the XDG Base Directory Specification; configuration is stored
under
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/adssh/
when this variable is set.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory for user data files
(default:
.BR ~/.local/share ).
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Base directory for user cache files
(default:
.BR ~/.cache ).
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/adssh/profile
XDG-preferred path for the login profile script (Starlark).
Loaded before the RC file when running as a login shell.
.TP
.B ~/.config/adssh/rc
XDG-preferred path for the interactive RC script (Starlark).
Evaluated at the start of every interactive session.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/
Legacy configuration directory.
.B ~/.adsshprofile
and
.B ~/.adsshrc
are the default RC paths when the XDG equivalents are absent.
The directory also holds
.BR audit.log ,
.BR history ,
.BR host_key ,
.BR authorized_keys ,
and
.BR policy.rego .
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Run an interactive shell session:
.nf
exec adssh
.fi
.TP
.B Start the built-in SSH server on port 2222:
.nf
adssh --serve :2222
.fi
.TP
.B Use adssh as a policy-gated SSH jump gateway:
.nf
ssh -J operator@adssh-gateway:2222 user@bastion.internal
.fi
.TP
.B Open a governed local TCP gateway:
.nf
gateway start --listen 127.0.0.1:0 --target bastion.internal:22 --name prod
gateway list
gateway stop gw-1
.fi
.TP
.B Lease a vault-backed secret into one command:
.nf
lease --from vault:secret/data/prod/deploy?field=token --as TOKEN -- deploy
.fi
.TP
.B Request break-glass elevation and explain a denial:
.nf
elevate request prod-admin --for 10m --reason "incident INC-1042"
??
.fi
.TP
.B Run a one-off command and exit (Starlark or shell auto-detected):
.nf
adssh -c 'ls -la | jq .'
adssh -c 'aws.ec2.list_instances(region="us-east-1")'
.fi
.TP
.B Launch a restricted, login shell with a custom policy:
.nf
adssh --login --restricted --policy /etc/adssh/policy.rego
.fi
